OpenAI REST API Documentation Guidelines
 
Version 1.0 | Last Updated: 2026-09-28
 
This document defines standards for writing, structuring, and maintaining consistent, accurate, and developer-friendly OpenAI REST API documentation.
 
 
 
1. Core Principles
 
- Clarity First: Use plain language; avoid ambiguity.
- Consistency: Uniform terminology, formatting, and structure across all endpoints.
- Completeness: Cover every parameter, error, and response variant.
- Actionability: Provide runnable examples and clear prerequisites.
- Security: Emphasize best practices for API key protection.
 
 
 
2. General Structure of API Pages
 
Every endpoint page follows this fixed order:
 
2.1 Endpoint Header
 
markdown  
# [Operation Name] — [Model/Resource Category]
`METHOD /v1/path/to/endpoint`
 
 
- Example:
 
markdown  
# Create Chat Completion — Chat API
`POST /v1/chat/completions`
 
 
2.2 Description
 
1–3 sentences explaining what it does and common use cases. Mention applicable models.
 
2.3 Authentication
 
🔑 Authentication: Bearer token in Authorization header.
 
http  
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY
 
 
- Also state: API keys must not be exposed in client-side code or public repos.
- Include: Organization header (optional):
 
http  
OpenAI-Organization: org-xxxxxx
 
 
2.4 Request Body / Parameters
 
Use tables with these columns:
 
Parameter Type Required Default Description 
 model  string ✅ Yes — ID of the model to use (e.g.,  gpt-4o ,  gpt-3.5-turbo ) 
 messages  array ✅ Yes — Conversation history in  role / content  format 
 temperature  number No 1 Sampling temperature 0–2; higher = more random 
 
Parameter Rules:
 
- Enum values: List all options explicitly →  ["json_object", "text"] 
- Range constraints: State min/max →  0 ≤ value ≤ 2 
- Default values: Always shown if applicable
- Arrays/objects: Define nested schema clearly
 
2.5 Request Example
 
Full, copy-pasteable code in cURL + Python + JavaScript minimum:
 
bash  
curl https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "model": "gpt-4o",
    "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Hello!"}],
    "temperature": 0.7
  }'
 
 
python  
from openai import OpenAI
client = OpenAI()

response = client.chat.completions.create(
  model="gpt-4o",
  messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Hello!"}]
)
print(response.choices[0].message.content)
 
 
2.6 Response
 
- Show full JSON sample
- Explain every top-level and nested field
- Include pagination fields where applicable ( has_more ,  after )
 
json  
{
  "id": "chatcmpl-abc123",
  "object": "chat.completion",
  "created": 1699000000,
  "model": "gpt-4o",
  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": "Hi! How can I help?"},
      "finish_reason": "stop"
    }
  ],
  "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 5, "completion_tokens": 10, "total_tokens": 15}
}
 
 
2.7 Error Codes
 
Standardized table mapping status codes to causes and fixes:
 
Status Code Error Type Typical Cause Resolution 
 401   authentication_error  Invalid/missing key Check key format; regenerate if leaked 
 429   rate_limit_exceeded  Too many requests Implement backoff/retry 
 500   server_error  OpenAI internal issue Retry with exponential backoff 
 400   invalid_request_error  Malformed params Validate request schema 
 
2.8 Additional Notes
 
- Model-specific limitations (context window, max tokens)
- Versioning / deprecation schedule
- Pricing links
- Rate-limit headers:  x-ratelimit-limit-requests , etc.
 
 
 
3. Naming & Terminology Standards
 
Rule Example 
Endpoints: Nouns, plural, kebab-case  /v1/files ,  /v1/models  ✅ — not  /v1/fileList  ❌ 
Parameters: snake_case  max_tokens ,  top_p  ✅ —  maxTokens  ❌ 
Response fields: snake_case  finish_reason  ✅ 
Operation verbs: Create / List / Retrieve / Modify / Delete "Create Embedding" ✅ 
Role values: exact lowercase match  system ,  user ,  assistant ,  tool  
 
 
 
4. Formatting Conventions
 
- Inline code: Backticks —  `model` 
- Keys/IDs: Always masked in examples —  sk-...xxxx 
- Versioning: Clearly label  /v1  vs future versions; note breaking changes
- Deprecation banner:
 
⚠️ DEPRECATED: This endpoint will be removed 2026-12-31. Migrate to  /v2/... 
 
 
 
5. Security & Compliance Guidelines
 
- Never place real API keys in examples — use  $OPENAI_API_KEY  or placeholder values
- Recommend: Store keys in env vars / secret managers, never in code
- Mention: IP restrictions, fine-grained keys, audit logs where applicable
- Link to privacy & data usage policy where relevant
 
 
 
6. Common Sections Across All Docs
 
1. Authentication — one-time overview page
2. Models List — updated as new models release
3. Rate Limits & Retry — exponential backoff formula + recommended retry codes
4. Error Handling — shared error object schema
5. Changelog — dated entries for all additions, changes, deprecations
 
 
 
7. Maintenance & Review Checklist
 
Before publishing updates:
 
All examples tested and working
Parameter table matches request schema exactly
Model names / version strings are current
No real credentials anywhere in the text
Links resolve correctly
Terminology consistent with earlier docs
Breaking changes announced with timeline
 
 
 
